<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">What does the Church teach about human sexuality?</p>


<p>
In 1979, a newly elected Pope walked into St. Peter's Square and began a series of Wednesday audiences that would transform Catholic teaching on the body. Over five years, Pope John Paul II delivered what became known as the "Theology of the Body." He asked a simple question: "What does it mean to be created male and female?" His answer was revolutionary: the body is not a machine or an object. It is a sacrament — a visible sign of the invisible mystery of God's love. "The body," he said, "and it alone, is capable of making visible what is invisible: the spiritual and divine." Human sexuality is not an accident or a drive to be indulged. It is a language of self-giving love, created by God to image His own fruitful, life-giving love.
</p>


</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Understanding the Teaching</h2>

<p>The Catechism of the Catholic Church provides clear guidance on how we are to live as followers of Christ. This teaching is not a burden but a path to true freedom and happiness.</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Activity</h4>
<p>Consider how this moral teaching applies to decisions you face daily.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Biblical Foundation</h2>

<p>Sacred Scripture reveals God's plan for human flourishing. The moral law is not arbitrary but reflects the wisdom of the Creator who knows what leads to human happiness.</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Connection</h4>
<p>Read the Scripture passages and reflect on how they illuminate this teaching.</p>
</div>

</section>

